let's dissect what's hurting China's  economy bloated state-owned companies  swelling public and private debt massive  over capacity in the property market and  heavy industry and that is just the  start at an aging population lack of  innovation environmental degradation and  deep-rooted corruption the battle to  contend with these challenges is playing  out in different ways across China's 31  provinces from the rich coastal regions  to the Rust Belt northeast and no one  knows all of this better than Chinese  leaders so here's the situation in 2013  China completed a once in a decade  transfer of power  xi Jinping replaced who Jintao as  president and promised to reform and  rebalance the economy under Xi's  leadership the government abandoned the  one-child policy cracked down on  bribe-taking and sped up steps to make  the currency more tradable but then this  happened the economy ran out of steam  last year growth slowed to 6.9 percent  its weakest pace in a quarter century as  the shift from exports and manufacturing  to services and consumption took its  toll jitters hit the booming stock  market triggering an epic bust that  wiped out 5 trillion dollars of value  and prompted the government to intervene  so what happened to embracing market  forces here's the argument on one side  the IMF and China's trading partners say  the country needs to loosen the guiding  hand of the state if it truly seeks  economic reform and on the face of it  Chinese leaders agree but then they go  and prop up the stock market revealing  an instinct to shelve all of those  reforms when the going gets tough  reining in financial excess and tackling  spiraling debt have become greater  priorities in the quest to protect  growth yet squeezed too hard and the  whole economy could founder
 

